# [Uncrossed Lines](https://leetcode.com/problems/uncrossed-lines/)

## Approach: Recursion + Memoization (LCS)

The problem is equivalent to finding the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)** of two arrays.

- If `nums1[i] == nums2[j]`, we can connect these elements and add `1` to the answer.
- Otherwise, we try skipping an element from either array and take the maximum result.
- We use memoization (`dp[i][j]`) to avoid recalculating overlapping subproblems.

### Code (C++)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int dp[501][501];

    int lcs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int i, int j) {
        if (i >= nums1.size() || j >= nums2.size()) return 0;

        if (dp[i][j] != -1) return dp[i][j];

        int take = 0, not_take = 0;

        if (nums1[i] == nums2[j]) {
            take = 1 + lcs(nums1, nums2, i + 1, j + 1);
        }

        not_take = max(
            lcs(nums1, nums2, i, j + 1),
            lcs(nums1, nums2, i + 1, j)
        );

        return dp[i][j] = max(take, not_take);
    }

    int maxUncrossedLines(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {
        memset(dp, -1, sizeof(dp));
        return lcs(nums1, nums2, 0, 0);
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n \times m)\), where \(n\) and \(m\) are the sizes of the two arrays.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n \times m)\) for the DP table, plus \(O(n+m)\) for the recursion stack in the worst case.

### Key Takeaway

Uncrossed lines cannot intersect, so matching elements must preserve their relative order. Therefore, the maximum number of uncrossed lines is exactly the length of the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**.
